      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hope Mills began as a lumber camp in 1766, with sawmill, grist mill, and pottery, thanks to water power and timber.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofhopemills.com/211/Our-Town-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1839 the first cotton mill powered by Hope Mills Dam was completed, launching the town’s textile era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hope_Mills%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By the mid‑1840s the Rockfish Factory had become North Carolina’s largest cotton mill by capital and production value.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofhopemills.com/211/Our-Town-History",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "General Sherman’s 1865 campaign burned eight cotton mills in Cumberland County, including the Rockfish Factory, reshaping the local economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofhopemills.com/211/Our-Town-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Cape Fear and Yadkin Valley Railway ran through Hope Mills in the late 1800s, later becoming part of the Atlantic Coast Line Railroad.",
